

3
00:01:42,890 --> 00:01:47,882
IN THE YEAR OF THE LORD 1247

4
00:02:54,882 --> 00:02:56,884
Do you have any food?

5
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:00,840
I am very hungry.

6
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:24,534
Blow your hair out.

7
00:03:26,541 --> 00:03:28,531
No one believes you're a boy.

8
00:03:31,710 --> 00:03:34,696
Blow your hair out.

9
00:03:45,052 --> 00:03:47,547
I'm not afraid.

10
00:03:47,662 --> 00:03:52,256
Wandering journeymen in the mountains
have told of the hero Robin Hood.

11
00:03:52,292 --> 00:03:54,436
He protects the meek.

12
00:04:01,541 --> 00:04:03,549
No one protects the meek.

13
00:04:05,170 --> 00:04:06,989
And he was no hero.

14
00:04:07,013 --> 00:04:10,481
He prays three times
a day and gives to the poor.

15
00:04:10,514 --> 00:04:13,794
And he has won
the heart of Lady Marian. Isn't he a hero?

16
00:04:13,819 --> 00:04:17,506
Lady Marian comes from
a robbery story told by a –

17
00:04:17,531 --> 00:04:20,438
– who was unfortunately even more drunk than me.

18
00:04:20,485 --> 00:04:23,735
She was Robin Hood's great love.

19
00:04:26,851 --> 00:04:30,835
There is no greater
love for someone like him.

20
00:04:32,908 --> 00:04:36,909
I know some people who have met
him. Do you want to hear a secret?

21
00:04:37,365 --> 00:04:39,366
Me too.

22
00:04:39,704 --> 00:04:43,536
He has never prayed so much as once.

23
00:04:43,540 --> 00:04:48,530
These rumors that
have spread are nothing but lies.

24
00:04:49,461 --> 00:04:52,110
He was a murderous robber –

25
00:04:52,187 --> 00:04:56,513
– who possibly slit the throat
of a sheriff among many others.

26
00:04:56,538 --> 00:04:59,549
People saw an opinion that didn't exist.

27
00:05:00,461 --> 00:05:02,266
He was no hero.

28
00:05:02,322 --> 00:05:06,312
He plundered and killed
only for pleasure.

29
00:05:10,834 --> 00:05:12,834
Those you met...

30
00:05:14,109 --> 00:05:18,109
Did they say he
regretted his misdeeds?

31
00:05:20,930 --> 00:05:23,921
He was probably tired.

32
00:05:26,133 --> 00:05:29,523
If you met the bandit Robin Hood –

33
00:05:29,610 --> 00:05:33,618
– you should hurry away and
save the life you now have left.

34
00:06:03,848 --> 00:06:06,846
and forgive us our sins.

35
00:06:08,257 --> 00:06:13,247
Through Jesus Christ our Lord. Amen.

36
00:07:19,468 --> 00:07:22,464
Who is your relative?

37
00:07:23,935 --> 00:07:28,243
William Wainwright of Pickering.

38
00:07:28,750 --> 00:07:30,744
Pickering...

39
00:07:32,185 --> 00:07:33,539
Far?

40
00:07:35,177 --> 00:07:37,174
Grandfather?

41
00:07:41,605 --> 00:07:46,592
You should have washed yourself
or waited for the wind to change.

42
00:07:48,673 --> 00:07:50,666
Or put your weight on the back leg.

43
00:07:52,981 --> 00:07:55,983
That's all. You did well.

44
00:09:17,139 --> 00:09:19,150
They sing lies –

45
00:09:19,925 --> 00:09:23,466
– these cheap fools.

46
00:09:23,491 --> 00:09:26,497
Lies about his heroism and goodness.

47
00:09:28,079 --> 00:09:30,080
But the bloodguilt is old...

48
00:09:31,313 --> 00:09:34,313
and generations of
his victims hunt him...

49
00:09:36,037 --> 00:09:38,741
because in reality –

50
00:09:38,766 --> 00:09:41,758
– was no one more evil and ruthless –

51
00:09:41,783 --> 00:09:45,787
– than the murderous bandit Robin Hood –

52
00:09:45,959 --> 00:09:48,946
– and his Little John.

53
00:09:52,012 --> 00:09:54,005
Talk to Thomas.

54
00:09:54,688 --> 00:09:58,691
– He was hanged down south. – Will?

55
00:09:59,858 --> 00:10:02,842
They ripped him open.

56
00:10:03,075 --> 00:10:05,085
I have no one else to ask.

57
00:10:06,945 --> 00:10:08,942
What happened?

58
00:10:10,695 --> 00:10:12,702
My name is Edward.

59
00:10:13,127 --> 00:10:15,122
Who was Edward?

60
00:10:16,127 --> 00:10:19,436
I met him on the road ten summers ago.

61
00:10:19,461 --> 00:10:24,186
His brother had died, and he wanted
to grow rye and peas on his land.

62
00:10:25,906 --> 00:10:28,894
No one knew what Edward looked like...

63
00:10:29,386 --> 00:10:33,379
So I killed him. Now I'm Edward.

64
00:10:34,546 --> 00:10:36,552
And Margaret is a good wife to me.

65
00:10:37,087 --> 00:10:41,086
– It's a good farm. – What happened?

66
00:10:42,002 --> 00:10:45,997
A family exposed me. They took my farm.

67
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:50,334
Took my Margaret. Tried to take my life.

68
00:10:53,024 --> 00:10:57,025
– We must fight for what
is mine. – It is not yours.

69
00:10:58,397 --> 00:11:00,408
It's my family.

70
00:11:04,756 --> 00:11:06,750
I'm tired, John.

71
00:11:07,721 --> 00:11:09,115
Edward.

72
00:11:11,490 --> 00:11:13,492
I'm tired, Edward.

73
00:11:18,816 --> 00:11:23,819
It will be a magnificent
battle. One for the adventures.

74
00:11:29,388 --> 00:11:32,397
– Are they strong? – Yes.

75
00:11:32,775 --> 00:11:37,457
They are from an old lineage.
Reportedly Viking blood.

76
00:11:38,590 --> 00:11:42,584
It is said that their great-grandfather once fought a bear.

77
00:11:43,425 --> 00:11:46,020
It's going to be a mighty blow, Robin.

78
00:11:49,117 --> 00:11:51,012
How many are they?

79
00:11:51,605 --> 00:11:53,611
Four, five...

80
00:11:53,981 --> 00:11:56,968
Together we can overcome them.

81
00:11:59,152 --> 00:12:01,159
We're probably going to die.

82
00:13:35,054 --> 00:13:37,042
Robin.

83
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:39,984
Robin!

84
00:13:40,887 --> 00:13:42,572
Bread?

85
00:13:45,023 --> 00:13:47,020
This is a good adventure.

86
00:13:48,355 --> 00:13:50,366
A good adventure.

87
00:13:52,992 --> 00:13:55,989
Do you remember when we met the potter?

88
00:13:56,118 --> 00:13:59,627
Did we leave him naked, or did we kill him?

89
00:13:59,652 --> 00:14:03,502
I tried to tell the story, and
someone said we left him naked.

90
00:14:03,527 --> 00:14:05,536
But I couldn't remember.

91
00:14:06,185 --> 00:14:09,178
You always told it well, Robin.

92
00:14:09,879 --> 00:14:12,862
Why would we leave him naked?

93
00:14:12,896 --> 00:14:14,906
I don't know.

94
00:14:16,008 --> 00:14:17,992
Just kidding.

95
00:14:18,824 --> 00:14:22,820
– We have never met a
potter. – What do you mean?

96
00:14:22,845 --> 00:14:27,831
It never happened. It's just
a story you heard somewhere.

97
00:14:28,812 --> 00:14:32,494
But if we had met a
potter, we would have killed him.

98
00:14:32,519 --> 00:14:34,806
I would remember if it was a story.

99
00:14:35,298 --> 00:14:36,300
Her.

100
00:14:37,161 --> 00:14:39,172
Tell me about your wife.

101
00:14:41,289 --> 00:14:42,736
Margaret.

102
00:14:44,687 --> 00:14:46,627
She is a good woman.

103
00:14:46,652 --> 00:14:49,652
A very good woman. She is God-fearing.

104
00:14:50,330 --> 00:14:53,344
– She teaches me piety. – Describe her.

105
00:14:55,242 --> 00:14:57,252
Should I describe her?

106
00:14:59,283 --> 00:15:01,282
Describe...

107
00:15:04,876 --> 00:15:07,863
Her hair is...

108
00:15:08,778 --> 00:15:09,782
red.

109
00:15:10,239 --> 00:15:13,892
– Red like? – Red like fresh blood.

110
00:15:14,127 --> 00:15:16,681
No, not blood.

111
00:15:16,706 --> 00:15:19,831
Something red that's good, just like her.

112
00:15:20,255 --> 00:15:22,260
Red as...

113
00:15:25,606 --> 00:15:28,517
the evening red.

114
00:15:29,166 --> 00:15:32,447
Like the red of summer evening.

115
00:15:32,472 --> 00:15:34,469
Margaret is a good wife...

116
00:15:35,095 --> 00:15:39,345
with hair like the red of summer evening.

117
00:15:39,755 --> 00:15:42,408
She cares about me –

118
00:15:42,433 --> 00:15:45,431
– and I from her.

119
00:15:52,314 --> 00:15:56,327
Robin, the land lies
open beyond the mountains.

120
00:15:58,245 --> 00:16:01,244
There are many places to start over.

121
00:16:04,878 --> 00:16:08,867
I don't intend to start over.

122
00:16:12,476 --> 00:16:14,911
I intend to die a proper death.

123
00:17:56,830 --> 00:17:58,830
Run, Hendrie!

124
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:03,217
– Run, Hendrie! – Edward!

125
00:18:03,242 --> 00:18:04,259
Go away! Hendrie!

126
00:18:04,262 --> 00:18:08,020
– Get the others! – Edward.

127
00:18:09,583 --> 00:18:12,257
My beloved.

128
00:18:13,187 --> 00:18:15,606
Take good care of little Margaret.

129
00:20:11,449 --> 00:20:14,369
Don't worry.

130
00:20:15,648 --> 00:20:19,635
You are safe. You
are safe, my daughter.

131
00:20:23,249 --> 00:20:25,258
Let us pray.

132
00:20:27,901 --> 00:20:30,424
Lord God –

133
00:20:30,950 --> 00:20:34,953
– grant us the grace to
love you with all our hearts.

134
00:20:35,151 --> 00:20:36,954
Pray with me.

135
00:20:37,182 --> 00:20:40,391
And when we find you, we will love you –

136
00:20:40,416 --> 00:20:45,908
– and hate the sins from which you have freed us.

137
00:20:45,951 --> 00:20:49,307
For Jesus Christ. Amen.

138
00:20:51,137 --> 00:20:54,000
Yes. Good.

139
00:22:05,958 --> 00:22:07,959
Sorry.

140
00:22:10,279 --> 00:22:12,290
Sorry.

141
00:22:13,010 --> 00:22:15,474
That's how it was when I woke up.

142
00:22:17,442 --> 00:22:20,433
That's how it was when I woke up.

143
00:22:20,458 --> 00:22:24,456
– Cousin. – That's how it was when I woke up.

144
00:22:25,316 --> 00:22:26,826
Sorry.

145
00:24:03,537 --> 00:24:07,428
We call upon you, O Lord.

146
00:24:07,463 --> 00:24:11,162
Show us mercy.

147
00:24:11,187 --> 00:24:13,188
And grant us forgiveness.

148
00:24:13,465 --> 00:24:17,469
O King of heaven and Lord of eternity.

149
00:24:17,550 --> 00:24:21,551
Hear our prayers as we offer them.

150
00:24:22,787 --> 00:24:25,100
And grant us forgiveness.

151
00:24:25,187 --> 00:24:29,424
Look to the sick, bring
the captive here –

152
00:24:29,487 --> 00:24:33,826
– help the widow and the orphan.

153
00:24:34,459 --> 00:24:37,279
And grant us forgiveness.

154
00:24:37,304 --> 00:24:41,693
We have sinned and gone astray.

155
00:24:41,725 --> 00:24:45,951
Will you, the savior of all –

156
00:24:45,976 --> 00:24:48,295
– save us?

157
00:24:48,321 --> 00:24:50,382
And grant us forgiveness.

158
00:24:50,407 --> 00:24:52,693
Show the penitent grace –

159
00:24:53,178 --> 00:24:57,464
– and wash away the shame of sin.

160
00:24:58,974 --> 00:25:01,162
And grant us forgiveness.

161
00:25:02,710 --> 00:25:05,092
Amen.

162
00:25:50,454 --> 00:25:52,449
Kill him!

163
00:25:58,460 --> 00:26:00,450
Be careful!

164
00:28:04,939 --> 00:28:08,571
Spare me!

165
00:28:39,843 --> 00:28:44,395
– Margaret! – No!

166
00:28:47,442 --> 00:28:50,441
John, no!

167
00:28:54,694 --> 00:28:57,692
Little John!

168
00:29:04,426 --> 00:29:08,723
Twenty winters ago
you murdered my cousin –

169
00:29:08,785 --> 00:29:11,467
– Walter of Hathersage.

170
00:29:11,832 --> 00:29:17,277
You have murdered my sons and my grandson.

171
00:29:17,465 --> 00:29:22,387
As a family settlement,
I accept your life.

172
00:29:22,460 --> 00:29:25,668
And your daughter's life!

173
00:31:28,282 --> 00:31:34,715
–... murderous acts...
–... cruel crimes...

174
00:31:36,473 --> 00:31:42,462
murderer of mother,
woman and child, for all eternity.

175
00:32:00,463 --> 00:32:05,435
No...!

176
00:32:52,408 --> 00:32:56,404
Your punishment awaits, Robin Hood.

177
00:32:56,429 --> 00:33:00,685
You killed my sisters and brothers.

178
00:33:10,555 --> 00:33:14,535
– Robin. – Punishment awaits, Robin Hood.

179
00:33:15,655 --> 00:33:17,655
Robin.

180
00:33:17,862 --> 00:33:22,021
I've heard stories about
a monastery far off the beaten track.

181
00:33:22,854 --> 00:33:26,614
The prioress is older than anyone can remember.

182
00:33:27,505 --> 00:33:30,349
She possesses healing power.

183
00:33:30,462 --> 00:33:34,464
I'll take you out to her.
She'll make you well again.

184
00:33:35,459 --> 00:33:38,455
I've heard incredible stories.

185
00:34:38,292 --> 00:34:42,295
Robin, we have to leave you.

186
00:34:42,461 --> 00:34:44,470
They're coming after us.

187
00:35:20,798 --> 00:35:22,797
That's right.

188
00:35:25,327 --> 00:35:28,326
Unusually so late.

189
00:35:31,235 --> 00:35:33,829
But it won't stay put.

190
00:35:36,227 --> 00:35:38,891
The long day is not that cold.

191
00:36:30,864 --> 00:36:32,878
A man came with you.

192
00:36:33,639 --> 00:36:38,154
He closed your wounds
with fire, and not unevenly.

193
00:36:38,819 --> 00:36:41,813
You've been here for six days.

194
00:36:41,838 --> 00:36:44,068
You have a stomach ulcer.

195
00:36:44,465 --> 00:36:48,454
Severe fractures of the ribs and one leg.

196
00:36:49,342 --> 00:36:52,271
Can you understand my words?

197
00:36:55,202 --> 00:36:57,200
Where are we?

198
00:36:57,461 --> 00:36:59,461
In the monastery of Saint Clement –

199
00:36:59,464 --> 00:37:02,162
– where you can recover.

200
00:37:02,187 --> 00:37:04,176
Which city?

201
00:37:04,457 --> 00:37:06,701
There is no city.

202
00:37:07,627 --> 00:37:10,611
Do you know the story of Saul?

203
00:37:11,464 --> 00:37:12,873
What?

204
00:37:12,898 --> 00:37:16,433
They say that there
was once a man named Saul –

205
00:37:16,458 --> 00:37:20,303
– who threatened the Lord's disciples with murder.

206
00:37:20,328 --> 00:37:25,889
He asked the high priest for
letters to Damascus, to the synagogues –

207
00:37:25,914 --> 00:37:31,053
– to imprison both men
and women who belonged to the Way.

208
00:37:32,390 --> 00:37:36,926
That's enough. Just a few drops.

209
00:37:39,294 --> 00:37:41,567
It wasn't too painful, was it?

210
00:37:42,944 --> 00:37:45,943
It wasn't.

211
00:37:48,792 --> 00:37:50,799
My name is Sister Bridget.

212
00:37:51,303 --> 00:37:53,037
What is your name?

213
00:37:54,767 --> 00:37:56,184
Randolph.

214
00:38:00,334 --> 00:38:03,014
I owe you an apology.

215
00:38:03,466 --> 00:38:05,455
What is that supposed to say?

216
00:38:07,357 --> 00:38:10,029
You begged me to let you die.

217
00:38:11,116 --> 00:38:13,119
And I didn't.

218
00:38:14,813 --> 00:38:17,813
– You said it was the
right thing. – I had a fever.

219
00:38:19,154 --> 00:38:21,490
You don't have that anymore.

220
00:38:22,414 --> 00:38:24,779
Rest now, Randolph.

221
00:38:47,314 --> 00:38:51,304
Your body is covered with scars.

222
00:39:07,340 --> 00:39:10,842
Do you wish to harm anyone in the monastery?

223
00:39:13,756 --> 00:39:15,146
I don't.

224
00:39:25,340 --> 00:39:28,330
Does evil follow you out here?

225
00:39:30,087 --> 00:39:33,081
It doesn't.

226
00:39:40,326 --> 00:39:43,990
You're not hurting them.

227
00:40:15,622 --> 00:40:17,637
And then back to bed.

228
00:40:32,780 --> 00:40:34,785
And then back to the door.

229
00:41:02,200 --> 00:41:04,258
Then open the door.

230
00:41:22,626 --> 00:41:25,636
Go as far as you want.

231
00:43:22,138 --> 00:43:24,139
Can you row?

232
00:43:26,247 --> 00:43:29,250
There is a boat on the shore to the west.

233
00:43:30,796 --> 00:43:36,245
But you're welcome to stay
if you help in the orchard.

234
00:43:36,270 --> 00:43:38,285
I am not a farmer.

235
00:43:38,788 --> 00:43:40,629
Then you don't know how to hunt.

236
00:43:41,054 --> 00:43:42,639
Yes, I do.

237
00:43:43,596 --> 00:43:46,599
There is wildlife here on the island.

238
00:43:46,624 --> 00:43:50,307
But you can't go hunting. You
can't even string a bow.

239
00:43:50,332 --> 00:43:52,600
– I can do that. – It's mostly rabbits.

240
00:43:52,625 --> 00:43:55,630
– Can you set traps? – Yes, I can.

241
00:43:56,976 --> 00:44:01,599
Good. But no more than six traps.
Otherwise the population will perish.

242
00:44:01,624 --> 00:44:03,637
Can you handle six traps?

243
00:44:07,627 --> 00:44:09,627
I can handle six traps.

244
00:44:11,350 --> 00:44:12,616
Well.

245
00:44:13,935 --> 00:44:17,726
Then you can help in
the orchard the rest of the time.

246
00:44:53,043 --> 00:44:55,055
Are you trying to escape?

247
00:44:58,624 --> 00:45:00,626
You are quiet.

248
00:45:01,624 --> 00:45:04,610
I am quiet and slow.

249
00:45:05,168 --> 00:45:07,175
You were quiet –

250
00:45:07,200 --> 00:45:09,990
– but you also have to be
slow. You are a cripple.

251
00:45:10,015 --> 00:45:12,027
And you are a leper.

252
00:45:12,625 --> 00:45:16,487
You can go
slow. We're safe here.

253
00:45:16,567 --> 00:45:21,406
– That's a bit of a statement. You know
? – Yes, because she has given us security.

254
00:45:21,431 --> 00:45:23,429
Priorind?

255
00:45:24,933 --> 00:45:26,556
She was like us.

256
00:45:27,128 --> 00:45:31,378
She experienced great sorrow, great pain.

257
00:45:32,062 --> 00:45:34,058
Became a widow at a very young age.

258
00:45:35,320 --> 00:45:39,314
But in her grief
she found the strength to heal us.

259
00:45:39,339 --> 00:45:42,325
You don't look healed.

260
00:45:45,338 --> 00:45:49,544
The prioress once told me
a story about a great philosopher.

261
00:45:50,340 --> 00:45:53,344
The greatest philosopher of his time.

262
00:45:53,958 --> 00:45:57,313
But one day his king turned against him –

263
00:45:57,338 --> 00:46:01,343
– and threw him into a dungeon where
he was to be tortured and executed.

264
00:46:01,932 --> 00:46:05,935
But in the philosopher's misery,
when he had nothing –

265
00:46:05,960 --> 00:46:09,278
– an angel came to him and spoke to him.

266
00:46:10,466 --> 00:46:14,950
And from her he learned that
it didn't matter –

267
00:46:14,975 --> 00:46:17,978
– if the whole world were pure misery...

268
00:46:19,335 --> 00:46:22,323
and only death awaited.

269
00:46:23,103 --> 00:46:28,107
Because he had one thing no one
could touch or take away from him.

270
00:46:29,978 --> 00:46:31,977
He had his mind.

271
00:46:33,114 --> 00:46:35,119
And in his mind...

272
00:46:36,215 --> 00:46:38,221
He had God.

273
00:46:39,907 --> 00:46:43,466
It's never too late to find peace.

274
00:46:44,864 --> 00:46:48,503
– What do you do? – I row every day.

275
00:46:48,528 --> 00:46:52,472
Not everyone can transport people like
your friend, so I sail them.

276
00:46:52,505 --> 00:46:55,341
– Will you sail me over? – No.

277
00:46:55,387 --> 00:46:58,434
You can row yourself when you
have regained your strength.

278
00:46:58,497 --> 00:47:01,334
Now watch your traps.

279
00:47:01,399 --> 00:47:04,391
– I'm going hunting. – You can't.

280
00:47:09,161 --> 00:47:13,919
Tell me, leper friend, what
happened to the great philosopher?

281
00:47:13,944 --> 00:47:16,938
He was then, of course,
tortured and executed.

282
00:47:18,330 --> 00:47:20,327
Of course.

283
00:49:22,451 --> 00:49:27,452
The leper has come
with someone from the distant shore.

284
00:50:06,445 --> 00:50:08,446
Father is dead.

285
00:51:23,273 --> 00:51:25,260
How is the girl doing?

286
00:51:38,335 --> 00:51:40,346
Now you should see my trees.

287
00:51:50,701 --> 00:51:53,361
It's elderberry.

288
00:51:53,467 --> 00:51:56,332
The berries should be picked late in the summer.

289
00:51:56,397 --> 00:51:58,405
Do they know what you used to be?

290
00:51:58,829 --> 00:52:02,186
Speak to me on this side
for the sake of my hearing.

291
00:52:04,340 --> 00:52:07,335
The pears will ripen immediately afterwards.

292
00:52:09,981 --> 00:52:14,975
– And what was I before?
– You were probably a mercenary.

293
00:52:15,000 --> 00:52:17,004
Yes, it's safe to say.

294
00:52:17,315 --> 00:52:19,943
We have a special appearance.

295
00:52:19,968 --> 00:52:23,021
– Do you remember the trees? – Pears and elder.

296
00:52:23,046 --> 00:52:25,051
Wonderful.

297
00:52:26,703 --> 00:52:28,715
Some of them know.

298
00:52:29,343 --> 00:52:33,331
But the prioress doesn't care
who we've been.

299
00:52:34,841 --> 00:52:37,461
The apples should be picked in the fall.

300
00:52:38,608 --> 00:52:42,601
Has she told you about what
happened to her?

301
00:52:43,475 --> 00:52:48,623
The prioress is taking care of the girl. Talk
to her if you are worried.

302
00:52:51,676 --> 00:52:53,676
What kind of trees were those?

303
00:52:54,897 --> 00:52:57,976
Pears, apples and elderberries.

304
00:52:59,209 --> 00:53:01,210
Wonderful.

305
00:53:12,070 --> 00:53:14,507
This is the last bloodletting.

306
00:53:15,288 --> 00:53:18,338
But your body is still far from healed.

307
00:53:21,249 --> 00:53:23,254
You are tired.

308
00:53:23,342 --> 00:53:25,332
Yes, that's me.

309
00:53:26,042 --> 00:53:28,568
But I've been tired before.

310
00:53:29,570 --> 00:53:32,561
The child is not sleeping.

311
00:53:34,427 --> 00:53:36,410
What has she been exposed to?

312
00:53:37,395 --> 00:53:39,393
Horrible things.

313
00:53:42,105 --> 00:53:45,210
– Has she told her
story? – She doesn't speak.

314
00:53:45,235 --> 00:53:48,225
She neither eats nor sleeps.

315
00:53:54,999 --> 00:53:56,585
What is her name?

316
00:54:03,978 --> 00:54:05,366
Margaret.

317
00:54:08,280 --> 00:54:09,850
Who is she?

318
00:54:11,855 --> 00:54:14,017
I met her father.

319
00:54:14,042 --> 00:54:15,063
A farmer.

320
00:54:15,921 --> 00:54:18,913
– He's dead. – Obviously.

321
00:54:19,160 --> 00:54:22,168
– And her mother? – She's probably dead too.

322
00:54:22,193 --> 00:54:25,318
– How? –
I don't know. She walked away.

323
00:54:26,046 --> 00:54:28,036
Was she killed?

324
00:54:28,584 --> 00:54:30,109
Perhaps.

325
00:54:34,995 --> 00:54:36,993
You are good at lying.

326
00:54:45,858 --> 00:54:47,554
Did the child see that?

327
00:54:49,663 --> 00:54:51,331
It is possible.

328
00:54:53,699 --> 00:54:56,273
It's devastating for a child to see.

329
00:55:05,477 --> 00:55:08,461
I feel bad for your friend.

330
00:55:11,830 --> 00:55:13,137
Not.

331
00:55:14,197 --> 00:55:16,630
Should I pray for him?

332
00:55:20,468 --> 00:55:22,465
If you want.

333
00:55:25,342 --> 00:55:27,344
What was his name?

334
00:55:31,132 --> 00:55:33,122
Edward.

335
00:55:34,541 --> 00:55:36,545
Edward...

336
00:55:39,239 --> 00:55:43,950
Let not your heart be troubled:
believe in God, believe also in me.

337
00:55:45,155 --> 00:55:48,747
In my Father's house there are many mansions.

338
00:55:49,512 --> 00:55:52,335
And when I have prepared
a place for you –

339
00:55:52,338 --> 00:55:56,098
– I will come again and take you to me –

340
00:55:56,123 --> 00:55:59,711
– so that you also may be where I am.

341
00:56:01,514 --> 00:56:05,500
And where I go, you know the way.

342
00:56:25,046 --> 00:56:28,150
– She has a knife. – She cut me!

343
00:56:28,479 --> 00:56:30,474
Margaret.

344
00:56:32,658 --> 00:56:34,658
Don't worry.

345
00:56:35,076 --> 00:56:39,727
Margaret, just relax.

346
00:56:42,690 --> 00:56:46,219
She doesn't know the island.
There are tidal waves.

347
00:56:51,247 --> 00:56:54,688
Margaret!

348
00:56:54,713 --> 00:56:56,729
Margaret!

349
00:58:55,321 --> 00:58:56,424
Aflever.

350
00:59:10,163 --> 00:59:12,165
Aflever.

351
00:59:28,431 --> 00:59:31,418
The men who hurt you...

352
00:59:33,382 --> 00:59:35,378
Do they know you're here?

353
00:59:48,163 --> 00:59:50,173
What is my name?

354
00:59:55,422 --> 00:59:57,414
Say my name.

355
00:59:58,891 --> 01:00:00,902
Randolph.

356
01:00:05,787 --> 01:00:07,068
Just exactly.

357
01:00:12,186 --> 01:00:14,287
Who is Little John?

358
01:00:16,201 --> 01:00:19,421
That name is nonsense
and should never be mentioned.

359
01:00:19,482 --> 01:00:23,623
If you mention it, the cat will come
at night and bite your tongue off.

360
01:00:33,424 --> 01:00:35,418
Don't worry.

361
01:00:37,568 --> 01:00:39,565
Go to sleep.

362
01:00:46,388 --> 01:00:49,375
I'm keeping an eye on the storm.

363
01:00:52,066 --> 01:00:54,621
Lie down.

364
01:00:57,926 --> 01:00:59,924
Close your eyes.

365
01:01:04,245 --> 01:01:06,240
Sleep safe.

366
01:01:10,540 --> 01:01:12,527
Sleep.

367
01:01:50,423 --> 01:01:53,003
Thank you for bringing her back.

368
01:01:54,807 --> 01:01:56,816
It was so little.

369
01:02:01,550 --> 01:02:04,421
Do you know the history of the monastery?

370
01:02:04,502 --> 01:02:06,492
The best.

371
01:02:08,417 --> 01:02:13,894
In prehistoric times
it was the sanctuary of the Druids.

372
01:02:15,331 --> 01:02:17,785
They worshipped unknown gods.

373
01:02:19,028 --> 01:02:22,024
Worship them out here.

374
01:02:22,049 --> 01:02:25,566
But then the Romans came and
sensed the magic here –

375
01:02:25,602 --> 01:02:28,592
– so they built their temples.

376
01:02:29,421 --> 01:02:33,449
But people after people have sensed
it and have come to worship...

377
01:02:34,785 --> 01:02:39,421
Countless gods and tribes who
wouldn't even be able to talk to each other.

378
01:02:40,603 --> 01:02:43,605
And yet we are all
drawn towards this place.

379
01:02:45,177 --> 01:02:48,175
Do you believe there is magic on the island?

380
01:02:49,902 --> 01:02:52,899
I believe it possesses a power.

381
01:02:54,381 --> 01:02:57,379
And I have seen the power of stories.

382
01:02:59,404 --> 01:03:03,412
They can be used to make
people commit atrocities.

383
01:03:03,946 --> 01:03:08,266
And then those
atrocities become stories...

384
01:03:08,907 --> 01:03:10,915
and stories.

385
01:03:14,418 --> 01:03:18,421
No. You shouldn't believe any of them.

386
01:03:18,514 --> 01:03:21,118
We can't take care of them.

387
01:03:22,805 --> 01:03:26,680
Knives can cut bread as well as meat.

388
01:03:27,337 --> 01:03:29,428
It's about balance.

389
01:03:30,056 --> 01:03:33,057
Does balance matter?

390
01:03:34,259 --> 01:03:37,266
If balance meant nothing –

391
01:03:37,551 --> 01:03:40,538
– we wouldn't be here.

392
01:03:41,421 --> 01:03:43,593
This world wouldn't...

393
01:03:43,618 --> 01:03:45,907
be here, and...

394
01:03:46,740 --> 01:03:52,721
we wouldn't exchange those words.
Have you ever considered that?

395
01:03:53,509 --> 01:03:58,424
The astonishing
precision of the balance over such an immense period of time.

396
01:03:59,180 --> 01:04:03,419
The balance it takes to
create any word –

397
01:04:03,422 --> 01:04:06,418
– which can come out between two lips and...

398
01:04:08,079 --> 01:04:10,915
understood by two minds.

399
01:04:17,782 --> 01:04:19,788
Randolph.

400
01:04:23,016 --> 01:04:24,540
Not.

401
01:05:43,299 --> 01:05:45,291
Aren't you hungry?

402
01:05:46,347 --> 01:05:48,343
Aren't you?

403
01:06:20,275 --> 01:06:22,850
I'm going to die soon.

404
01:06:23,420 --> 01:06:25,410
How do you know that?

405
01:06:26,908 --> 01:06:29,717
I can feel it inside.

406
01:06:31,423 --> 01:06:33,631
How does it feel?

407
01:06:35,300 --> 01:06:37,301
It feels right.

408
01:06:48,687 --> 01:06:50,688
There is someone.

409
01:06:53,093 --> 01:06:55,425
It has been busy on the lake.

410
01:06:58,291 --> 01:07:01,294
First around the feet. Small cuts.

411
01:07:01,421 --> 01:07:03,423
Good. And the other one.

412
01:07:10,830 --> 01:07:12,275
Well.

413
01:07:12,415 --> 01:07:15,414
And now in front, down through the stomach.

414
01:07:15,417 --> 01:07:19,009
Good. No.

415
01:07:20,024 --> 01:07:21,389
Look here.

416
01:07:21,414 --> 01:07:24,532
Such.

417
01:07:24,556 --> 01:07:30,289
Take the second one. Like this.

418
01:08:05,860 --> 01:08:07,892
You are a brave young man.

419
01:08:08,940 --> 01:08:10,944
Thank you, sister.

420
01:08:12,160 --> 01:08:14,282
Are you hungry, Arthur?

421
01:08:17,128 --> 01:08:19,188
Yes, sister.

422
01:08:19,213 --> 01:08:24,485
Sarah is making
you a hearty meal tonight. Stewed herring.

423
01:08:26,093 --> 01:08:28,114
– Do you like it? – Yes.

424
01:08:28,813 --> 01:08:30,821
Where are you from, Arthur?

425
01:08:34,181 --> 01:08:37,282
– My family farm in
the Keswick area. – Keswick?

426
01:08:37,306 --> 01:08:38,828
– Ja, hr. – Randolph.

427
01:08:38,853 --> 01:08:41,141
Randolph... In the Keswick area.

428
01:08:41,167 --> 01:08:43,681
The leper says
you're looking for your niece.

429
01:08:43,768 --> 01:08:46,126
– Yes, I do. – Were you assaulted?

430
01:08:47,843 --> 01:08:49,848
– Yes. – On the road?

431
01:08:51,163 --> 01:08:53,391
– Yes. – You're safe here.

432
01:08:53,416 --> 01:08:55,411
Which way?

433
01:08:56,234 --> 01:08:58,236
The road to Keswick, Mr.

434
01:08:59,322 --> 01:09:02,323
– Praise the Lord that you
survived. – I do.

435
01:09:02,419 --> 01:09:06,419
How about pears in honey
for dessert for your courage?

436
01:09:08,483 --> 01:09:10,479
Thank you, sister.

437
01:09:24,495 --> 01:09:25,851
Little Margaret...

438
01:09:27,154 --> 01:09:32,055
That Arthur... did you see
him before you came out to the monastery?

439
01:09:34,973 --> 01:09:36,969
Margaret.

440
01:09:43,198 --> 01:09:45,195
Those men...

441
01:09:45,331 --> 01:09:48,320
The men who hurt your father...

442
01:09:49,367 --> 01:09:51,355
Did you see their faces?

443
01:09:52,184 --> 01:09:54,182
Dad said I should run.

444
01:10:04,759 --> 01:10:06,751
Dad stopped praying.

445
01:10:07,853 --> 01:10:09,610
He was angry.

446
01:10:10,605 --> 01:10:13,009
He prayed in his mind.

447
01:10:15,223 --> 01:10:17,222
What is dad's name?

448
01:10:20,821 --> 01:10:22,829
Your father is your father.

449
01:10:34,838 --> 01:10:36,509
Come here with it.

450
01:10:45,639 --> 01:10:48,623
Beautiful craftsmanship.

451
01:10:53,426 --> 01:10:56,422
Can you see it bends more here?

452
01:10:57,282 --> 01:11:01,196
It must be smoothed out so that it bends evenly.

453
01:11:02,009 --> 01:11:05,563
This piece needs to be sliced ​​thinner.

454
01:11:10,892 --> 01:11:12,892
Can you make one for me?

455
01:11:15,245 --> 01:11:17,290
Should I make you a bow?

456
01:11:21,340 --> 01:11:22,673
Excellent.

457
01:11:30,668 --> 01:11:34,657
Today I was reminded of a
story by the poet Lucretius.

458
01:11:36,419 --> 01:11:39,423
He wrote about something called atoms.

459
01:11:40,218 --> 01:11:45,789
If you separate trees, people and stones –

460
01:11:45,822 --> 01:11:51,165
– it is the smallest,
invisible parts that make up...

461
01:11:53,052 --> 01:11:55,374
everything in creation.

462
01:11:55,399 --> 01:11:59,398
And these atoms float
through the void and...

463
01:12:00,171 --> 01:12:03,751
sometimes changes course for no reason.

464
01:12:06,284 --> 01:12:09,282
The tiniest little bit.

465
01:12:13,397 --> 01:12:15,811
And if not that...

466
01:12:15,836 --> 01:12:19,089
small unpredictable change –

467
01:12:19,114 --> 01:12:21,545
– forced the atoms to –

468
01:12:21,570 --> 01:12:24,570
– to intertwine with each other...

469
01:12:27,161 --> 01:12:30,444
nothing under heaven would exist.

470
01:15:14,165 --> 01:15:16,167
Good. Harder.

471
01:15:17,720 --> 01:15:19,494
Like this. Good.

472
01:15:24,112 --> 01:15:27,113
Harder. Now I'm going to help you.

473
01:15:28,533 --> 01:15:30,525
Day the day of the.

474
01:15:30,833 --> 01:15:34,743
Good. That's how it should be. Good.

475
01:15:37,819 --> 01:15:39,828
Which one is best for your bow?

476
01:15:39,853 --> 01:15:41,855
Let's take a look.

477
01:15:44,365 --> 01:15:47,353
They are both nice.

478
01:15:57,814 --> 01:16:01,226
– Margaret! – Come and play!

479
01:16:01,251 --> 01:16:03,814
Let's play tag. You count.

480
01:16:03,839 --> 01:16:07,720
– You can do that. – No, it's your turn.

481
01:16:41,514 --> 01:16:43,499
Arthur...

482
01:16:43,963 --> 01:16:46,423
– Can you set traps? – Yes.

483
01:16:46,774 --> 01:16:51,754
Good. I need
someone to take over. Come on.

484
01:17:02,472 --> 01:17:04,471
Does your family have land?

485
01:17:05,656 --> 01:17:07,646
Yes.

486
01:17:08,674 --> 01:17:10,670
How many are you?

487
01:17:12,250 --> 01:17:14,247
I don't know.

488
01:17:20,432 --> 01:17:22,444
Is everything okay?

489
01:17:23,007 --> 01:17:25,062
Yes, yes.

490
01:17:25,632 --> 01:17:29,461
I'm not as young as I once was.

491
01:17:29,580 --> 01:17:31,694
I could use a hand.

492
01:17:36,194 --> 01:17:37,492
Not.

493
01:17:38,738 --> 01:17:41,729
It was a bit of a blow otherwise.

494
01:17:42,796 --> 01:17:44,795
– Yes. – Who was it?

495
01:17:46,757 --> 01:17:48,483
A man.

496
01:17:48,487 --> 01:17:50,585
Some men.

497
01:17:50,616 --> 01:17:53,492
– Bandits. – A man? Bandits?

498
01:17:53,768 --> 01:17:56,351
– Yes. – Can I see?

499
01:18:01,493 --> 01:18:03,484
That's a real blow.

500
01:18:04,616 --> 01:18:07,501
– You're lucky to have
survived, kid. – Yes.

501
01:18:07,503 --> 01:18:11,481
– Be grateful
to be alive. – Yes.

502
01:18:11,484 --> 01:18:13,501
He used a staff.

503
01:18:20,524 --> 01:18:24,520
You're lucky he didn't
cut your head off.

504
01:18:26,776 --> 01:18:28,766
Randolph!

505
01:18:31,065 --> 01:18:33,062
Randolph!

506
01:18:35,505 --> 01:18:37,505
Moshe!

507
01:18:40,400 --> 01:18:43,624
The prioress says you must come!

508
01:19:24,488 --> 01:19:26,491
Are you in severe pain?

509
01:19:35,713 --> 01:19:38,700
Do you remember the trees?

510
01:19:40,416 --> 01:19:43,417
Pears, apples and elderberries.

511
01:19:44,816 --> 01:19:46,814
Wonderful.

512
01:19:56,754 --> 01:19:59,692
You are the most important person –

513
01:19:59,759 --> 01:20:02,752
– in little Margaret's life.

514
01:20:03,949 --> 01:20:06,943
I'm not important.

515
01:20:17,617 --> 01:20:21,306
Think of all those whose lives you have crossed.

516
01:20:24,417 --> 01:20:27,877
We matter to all of them.

517
01:20:34,665 --> 01:20:38,586
Do you know the story of Guy of Gisborne...

518
01:20:39,790 --> 01:20:42,187
and Robin Hood?

519
01:20:46,490 --> 01:20:47,681
Yes.

520
01:20:52,751 --> 01:20:55,299
They say you killed him.

521
01:20:56,916 --> 01:21:00,285
– Did you really behead him? –
You speak in delusion, leper friend.

522
01:21:00,310 --> 01:21:03,300
I don't say a word to anyone.

523
01:21:04,493 --> 01:21:08,603
But you shouldn't lie to
a man on his deathbed.

524
01:21:10,166 --> 01:21:11,490
So...

525
01:21:11,493 --> 01:21:14,489
Did you really behead him?

526
01:21:17,252 --> 01:21:19,482
There was no Guy of Gisborne.

527
01:21:19,563 --> 01:21:23,551
It was just one of many stories.

528
01:21:24,811 --> 01:21:27,800
But I have beheaded others.

529
01:21:34,431 --> 01:21:36,885
You really don't remember?

530
01:21:38,712 --> 01:21:40,490
The best.

531
01:21:40,890 --> 01:21:43,892
Think about the name.

532
01:21:47,328 --> 01:21:50,325
The gentleman Guy of Gisborne.

533
01:21:53,486 --> 01:21:55,775
But you didn't behead him.

534
01:21:58,068 --> 01:22:00,228
You cut off his ear.

535
01:22:07,487 --> 01:22:09,377
You said –

536
01:22:09,437 --> 01:22:13,010
– that in another life
you would have toasted him.

537
01:22:14,626 --> 01:22:16,634
Do you remember that?

538
01:22:28,314 --> 01:22:31,838
We have apparently found another life.

539
01:22:38,424 --> 01:22:43,572
There is one atrocity I
would ask you not to commit.

540
01:22:46,377 --> 01:22:50,484
You must tend the orchard
until you too die.

541
01:22:53,579 --> 01:22:56,567
Serving the Prioress...

542
01:22:57,505 --> 01:23:01,505
and people who come and go to the monastery.

543
01:23:05,799 --> 01:23:09,796
But at no time...

544
01:23:12,154 --> 01:23:14,155
in these...

545
01:23:14,488 --> 01:23:17,492
good and peaceful years...

546
01:23:19,751 --> 01:23:23,760
You must tell the prioress
who you really are.

547
01:23:29,291 --> 01:23:30,498
Why?

548
01:23:32,824 --> 01:23:34,828
Because...

549
01:23:36,032 --> 01:23:41,556
one of the nameless and faceless
dead in the dark recesses of your mind...

550
01:23:44,280 --> 01:23:46,556
was in another life...

551
01:23:48,689 --> 01:23:50,923
her fiancé.

552
01:23:58,491 --> 01:24:00,502
Promise me...

553
01:24:04,999 --> 01:24:06,791
Robin.

554
01:24:08,006 --> 01:24:10,049
Promise me.

555
01:25:41,118 --> 01:25:43,088
Goodbye, Bridget.

556
01:25:56,021 --> 01:25:57,299
Arthur.

557
01:25:59,361 --> 01:26:00,752
Come on.

558
01:26:03,742 --> 01:26:06,267
There is a child on the distant shore.

559
01:26:07,001 --> 01:26:08,744
Maybe it's your niece.

560
01:26:10,585 --> 01:26:13,924
The leper said you
're looking for your missing niece.

561
01:26:14,440 --> 01:26:15,663
Yes.

562
01:26:16,305 --> 01:26:20,017
– Could it be
her? – We'll see.

563
01:26:58,000 --> 01:27:00,982
I can't see anyone.

564
01:27:01,440 --> 01:27:03,437
We are waiting.

565
01:27:03,462 --> 01:27:05,471
Maybe they'll return.

566
01:27:27,265 --> 01:27:29,265
What is your name?

567
01:27:32,577 --> 01:27:33,838
Arthur.

568
01:27:34,889 --> 01:27:39,799
Don't waste your time lying
to me. What's your real name?

569
01:27:43,137 --> 01:27:45,137
Godwyn

570
01:27:47,653 --> 01:27:52,667
You are deciding, Godwyn,
whether to do the deed yourself –

571
01:27:53,427 --> 01:27:55,436
– or pick up your family.

572
01:27:58,368 --> 01:28:01,791
– What deed? – How many
have you killed, Godwyn?

573
01:28:03,128 --> 01:28:06,523
How many children have you killed?

574
01:28:11,296 --> 01:28:14,525
I've killed so many
that I can't count them.

575
01:28:16,001 --> 01:28:20,250
Longer than you have lived,
I have killed their relatives –

576
01:28:20,275 --> 01:28:23,055
– I killed an eternity
ago. I don't remember them.

577
01:28:23,080 --> 01:28:26,103
And they meant nothing to me, but...

578
01:28:26,688 --> 01:28:31,478
their brothers, children and
grandchildren remember me.

579
01:28:32,151 --> 01:28:34,298
So now I have to kill them.

580
01:28:35,072 --> 01:28:37,205
Time after time.

581
01:28:37,862 --> 01:28:41,861
In various shapes that shadow the path...

582
01:28:42,962 --> 01:28:46,943
ongoing, and it never stops.

583
01:28:48,642 --> 01:28:50,986
It's a curse, Godwyn.

584
01:28:52,360 --> 01:28:57,362
And you call it down upon
yourself, even if you do it out of duty.

585
01:28:57,560 --> 01:29:00,556
It doesn't take care of your duty.

586
01:29:00,581 --> 01:29:04,025
This world only cares about blood.

587
01:29:05,187 --> 01:29:08,195
And blood is the only thing it gives back.

588
01:29:10,209 --> 01:29:13,214
But do you want to kill a child, Godwyn?

589
01:29:15,490 --> 01:29:20,483
Do you want to take the blame for
the murder of a little girl?

590
01:29:22,865 --> 01:29:24,095
The best.

591
01:29:35,871 --> 01:29:37,857
Is your father dead?

592
01:29:38,913 --> 01:29:40,463
And your uncles?

593
01:29:43,211 --> 01:29:46,209
I am the oldest one left.

594
01:29:49,472 --> 01:29:51,468
Who else is alive?

595
01:29:53,416 --> 01:29:54,705
My mother.

596
01:29:56,185 --> 01:29:57,525
My aunts.

597
01:29:59,000 --> 01:30:02,985
– My sisters. – You have a home, Godwyn.

598
01:30:04,805 --> 01:30:06,838
– Yes. – Go home.

599
01:30:07,998 --> 01:30:11,510
Tell the family that the blood debt has been paid.

600
01:30:11,535 --> 01:30:14,533
They will all be paid one day.

601
01:30:15,282 --> 01:30:17,291
Sleep in your own bed.

602
01:30:17,824 --> 01:30:22,806
Cherish the life you
can still share with your loved ones.

603
01:30:23,625 --> 01:30:26,623
And if you return –

604
01:30:26,648 --> 01:30:30,799
– have you wasted this chance
to have a life that I am giving you now.

605
01:30:31,728 --> 01:30:36,266
And then I'll cut your throat
and never give you another thought.

606
01:30:42,151 --> 01:30:43,461
Go then.

607
01:30:45,878 --> 01:30:47,881
And don't look back.

608
01:31:41,766 --> 01:31:44,014
I thought you had left.

609
01:31:53,462 --> 01:31:56,363
I am Robin the Outlaw.

610
01:31:59,120 --> 01:32:03,644
You should know that. I am a monster.

611
01:32:04,912 --> 01:32:07,906
I am not someone named Randolph.

612
01:32:09,168 --> 01:32:12,613
I am the Outlaw, Robin Hood.

613
01:32:27,903 --> 01:32:29,409
Avoid...

614
01:34:29,296 --> 01:34:31,386
Tell me I have to travel...

615
01:34:33,502 --> 01:34:35,513
So I'm leaving now.

616
01:34:42,758 --> 01:34:44,394
Go to bed.

617
01:35:04,171 --> 01:35:06,438
– Bridget... – Lie down.

618
01:38:31,286 --> 01:38:34,281
You burned my husband alive.

619
01:38:38,823 --> 01:38:43,840
I dream that he was already dead,
but why else did you close the door?

620
01:38:47,536 --> 01:38:52,526
For what seemed like an eternity,
I searched the ashes of our home.

621
01:38:54,903 --> 01:38:57,212
And he looked so small.

622
01:38:58,798 --> 01:39:02,931
He was huddled in the corner,
and most of him was gone.

623
01:39:07,095 --> 01:39:10,102
But despite my dreams
, I know he was alive.

624
01:39:15,013 --> 01:39:20,018
Because to the very end,
he embraced our children.

625
01:40:07,978 --> 01:40:09,228
Randolph?

626
01:40:11,719 --> 01:40:13,493
It's me.

627
01:41:07,042 --> 01:41:10,064
People talk about Robin Hood.

628
01:41:13,985 --> 01:41:15,991
Tells his stories.

629
01:41:22,108 --> 01:41:24,110
It's all lies.

630
01:41:27,500 --> 01:41:30,495
Lies I told a long time ago...

631
01:41:32,052 --> 01:41:34,052
so the fools...

632
01:41:35,378 --> 01:41:37,388
followed me into the darkness.

633
01:41:46,652 --> 01:41:48,645
Robin.

634
01:41:54,363 --> 01:41:56,371
What have they done to you?

635
01:42:02,220 --> 01:42:04,229
Don't be afraid.

636
01:42:05,998 --> 01:42:08,997
We'll have to get you out of here.

637
01:42:10,665 --> 01:42:13,468
I'm saving little Margaret.

638
01:42:14,753 --> 01:42:18,369
And then we start over together.

639
01:42:18,394 --> 01:42:20,079
Edward.

640
01:42:23,650 --> 01:42:26,496
Tell me about your wife.

641
01:42:35,705 --> 01:42:37,704
My wife?

642
01:42:39,499 --> 01:42:41,486
Margaret.

643
01:42:45,215 --> 01:42:46,540
Margaret.

644
01:42:48,614 --> 01:42:51,525
My wife, Margaret.

645
01:42:53,005 --> 01:42:55,013
Describe her.

646
01:42:57,374 --> 01:42:59,095
Should I describe her?

647
01:43:02,796 --> 01:43:05,778
Her hair is...

648
01:43:06,382 --> 01:43:08,383
red like...

649
01:43:09,458 --> 01:43:11,650
the evening red.

650
01:43:14,274 --> 01:43:18,272
Like the red of summer evening.

651
01:43:24,572 --> 01:43:27,603
Red as...

652
01:43:27,628 --> 01:43:30,728
the evening red of summer.

653
01:43:49,186 --> 01:43:50,936
Robin.

654
01:43:56,546 --> 01:43:59,537
Have you found your hard death?

655
01:44:59,282 --> 01:45:02,271
I'm ready to end it.

656
01:45:08,572 --> 01:45:11,558
I owe you this life.

657
01:45:18,149 --> 01:45:20,159
It's horrible.

658
01:45:24,155 --> 01:45:26,819
But I owe you this life.

659
01:45:26,852 --> 01:45:29,146
You don't owe me anything.

660
01:45:33,599 --> 01:45:36,585
I have never taken a life.

661
01:45:40,420 --> 01:45:43,423
It can be like pruning a flower.

662
01:45:45,121 --> 01:45:48,928
– It shouldn't be.
– No, it shouldn't be.

663
01:45:50,705 --> 01:45:52,553
But it could be.

664
01:45:57,708 --> 01:46:01,693
I help those who come out here on the island.

665
01:46:02,927 --> 01:46:04,930
I heal them.

666
01:46:08,142 --> 01:46:10,141
I beg you.

667
01:46:14,898 --> 01:46:16,898
Heal me.

668
01:47:11,905 --> 01:47:13,701
Little Margaret.

669
01:47:19,241 --> 01:47:21,711
Your father's name was John.

670
01:47:30,901 --> 01:47:33,909
I knew him for many years –

671
01:47:34,390 --> 01:47:37,380
– before he became your father.

672
01:47:42,341 --> 01:47:44,163
I met him –

673
01:47:45,204 --> 01:47:48,014
– on the way over a bridge the other day...

674
01:47:48,712 --> 01:47:51,690
over a rushing river.

675
01:47:53,534 --> 01:47:56,522
He was a stubborn rad.

676
01:47:58,108 --> 01:48:00,703
He wouldn't bend.

677
01:48:02,295 --> 01:48:05,241
Not for anyone.

678
01:48:05,266 --> 01:48:07,280
Not even me.

679
01:48:10,083 --> 01:48:12,090
So we fought –

680
01:48:12,364 --> 01:48:14,366
– on that bridge.

681
01:48:15,709 --> 01:48:18,939
Went on the attack. Back and forth.

682
01:48:21,061 --> 01:48:24,059
I gave him one on the side of the head.

683
01:48:26,093 --> 01:48:28,993
He hit me in the stomach.

684
01:48:29,702 --> 01:48:32,700
And I was staggering on that bridge.

685
01:48:33,686 --> 01:48:35,517
Splash!

686
01:48:36,315 --> 01:48:38,822
I landed face down on the seagull.

687
01:48:42,412 --> 01:48:45,410
When I managed to drag myself up –

688
01:48:45,435 --> 01:48:47,438
– soaked...

689
01:48:48,979 --> 01:48:50,985
we embraced each other.

690
01:48:53,710 --> 01:48:56,111
We called your father –

691
01:48:56,198 --> 01:48:58,193
– Little John.

692
01:48:59,162 --> 01:49:02,158
And we lived for many years...

693
01:49:04,533 --> 01:49:08,518
like outlaws in the green forest.

694
01:49:10,960 --> 01:49:13,868
And we partied...

695
01:49:13,893 --> 01:49:14,939
We...

696
01:49:15,790 --> 01:49:19,774
We gave silver to the meek.

697
01:49:36,639 --> 01:49:38,960
But we had stolen the silver.

698
01:49:44,757 --> 01:49:46,751
We had stolen it...

699
01:49:47,651 --> 01:49:48,772
from...

700
01:49:49,452 --> 01:49:51,921
from evil people.

701
01:49:55,683 --> 01:49:57,681
Can you understand that?

702
01:49:59,530 --> 01:50:01,147
Evil people.

703
01:50:16,163 --> 01:50:17,365
From the lighthouse...

704
01:50:18,762 --> 01:50:21,350
didn't want
me to tell you...

705
01:50:26,628 --> 01:50:29,600
because he loved you so much.

706
01:50:33,020 --> 01:50:35,608
You must never tell anyone.

707
01:50:36,842 --> 01:50:39,405
Not even the children you play with.

708
01:50:40,920 --> 01:50:44,921
You must never mention your father's name.

709
01:50:48,846 --> 01:50:51,147
There are evil people out there.

710
01:50:53,828 --> 01:50:57,816
And they are angry with your father.

711
01:51:01,763 --> 01:51:04,752
And that's why they're angry with you.

712
01:51:08,952 --> 01:51:11,499
Can we keep that secret?

713
01:51:13,600 --> 01:51:16,708
I can keep that
secret, little Margaret.

714
01:51:18,819 --> 01:51:19,905
Can you?

715
01:51:24,649 --> 01:51:25,866
Well.

716
01:51:28,124 --> 01:51:30,132
Come to me.

717
01:51:39,756 --> 01:51:41,753
Day here.

718
01:51:43,035 --> 01:51:45,042
Why was he small?

719
01:51:45,349 --> 01:51:46,574
What?

720
01:51:47,533 --> 01:51:50,520
Why was my father called little?

721
01:51:55,621 --> 01:51:58,689
Because he was little when I found him.

722
01:51:59,631 --> 01:52:01,628
Like you.

723
01:52:09,084 --> 01:52:11,089
Put it on.

724
01:52:33,588 --> 01:52:37,579
Bend it with your leg like you saw me do.

725
01:52:53,356 --> 01:52:54,541
Well.

726
01:52:58,056 --> 01:52:59,669
Go to the window.

727
01:53:23,563 --> 01:53:24,999
Put an arrow on the bow.

728
01:53:33,541 --> 01:53:35,194
Find a point –

729
01:53:36,020 --> 01:53:39,038
– where the water meets the sky.

730
01:53:43,430 --> 01:53:46,423
Pull the arrow up to the mouth...

731
01:53:49,596 --> 01:53:51,601
and hold it.

732
01:53:52,851 --> 01:53:55,781
I say guy.

733
01:53:57,940 --> 01:53:59,946
And when I do...

734
01:54:00,388 --> 01:54:03,377
then you don't let go.

735
01:54:04,934 --> 01:54:07,939
You just don't hold on anymore.

736
01:54:12,234 --> 01:54:13,700
Well.

737
01:54:14,709 --> 01:54:16,809
Keep your eye on the target.

738
01:54:19,670 --> 01:54:22,654
Your body is moving all the time.

739
01:54:25,267 --> 01:54:27,261
Feel –

740
01:54:27,286 --> 01:54:30,298
– when the arrow takes flight.

741
01:54:32,770 --> 01:54:35,442
You find a dance.

742
01:54:37,358 --> 01:54:40,353
Hold on to it until I say, dude.

743
01:54:43,529 --> 01:54:45,538
You are strong.

744
01:54:46,198 --> 01:54:47,481
Note...

745
01:54:49,342 --> 01:54:51,350
it swayed.

746
01:54:54,179 --> 01:54:55,911
Den in.

747
01:55:02,708 --> 01:55:04,083
Guy.

748
01:56:25,693 --> 01:56:27,704
Translated by Lasse Schmidt Danish Video Text

